# Contract-major 2 inclusive ranges

Download this `.livemd` file, open it in [Livebook](https://livebook.dev), and choose
**Open → Import from file**, then **Run all**. Use Elixir 1.18 or later and OTP 27 or later
with access to public Hex. No secrets or external services are needed.

Limit a compute request to one through eight workers. Exercise both inclusive endpoints,
out-of-range integers, a mismatched float tag, and invalid selector inputs.

Every refusal uses a newly signed proof matching its request arguments, so the range selector
is what fails. The host still owns trusted keys, revocation, replay reservation, and authority.
Keys stay in memory; no processes, tables, or files are created.

[Open this 1.5.0 release notebook](https://livebook.dev/run?url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fbaselabs%2Fbounded_authority_protocol%2Fblob%2Fbaselabs_bounded_authority_protocol-v1.5.0%2Fdocs%2Fnotebooks%2Frange-selectors.livemd).
The launch opens this journey at its immutable release tag.

## Setup

Install the exact published package. Fixed evaluation times keep the results reproducible;
only safe semantic values are persisted, never keys, signatures, tokens, or nonces.

```elixir
Mix.install([{:bounded_authority_protocol, "== 1.5.0"}])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:ok
```

## Generate Ed25519 keys

Contract-major 2 uses Ed25519. Compute the holder thumbprint and define the signing operation
that will assemble each grant and proof.

```elixir
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V2
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.{Credentials, ExpectedRequest, TrustedIssuer}

{issuer_public, issuer_private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519)
{holder_public, holder_private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519)
{:ok, thumbprint} = BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.Jwk.public_key_thumbprint_raw(holder_public, %{})

now = 1_800_000_100

sign = fn input, key ->
  signature = :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :none, input.message, [key, :ed25519])
  {:ok, compact} = V2.assemble_compact(input, signature)
  compact
end

:signer_ready
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:signer_ready
```

## Convert cast request arguments

Cast each argument to the operation's declared type before tagging it. The shared term converter
preserves whole floats, converts atom member names, and refuses colliding names. The decoder's
structural and numeric limits apply; raw-text limits do not apply to already-parsed terms.

```elixir
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.Json

{:ok, {:object, [{"workers", {:integer, 4}}]}} = Json.from_term(%{workers: 4}, %{})
{:ok, {:object, [{"workers", {:float, 4.0}}]}} = Json.from_term(%{workers: 4.0}, %{})
{:error, :invalid} = Json.from_term(%{:workers => 4, "workers" => 4}, %{})

[:integer_preserved, :float_preserved, :colliding_names_rejected]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[:integer_preserved, :float_preserved, :colliding_names_rejected]
```

## Build and sign a bounded grant

Both range selectors must hold. Integer bounds require integer-tagged arguments; numeric
equality does not convert a float tag into an integer tag.

```elixir
grant = %V2.Grant{
  key_id: "issuer-1",
  issuer: "https://issuer.example",
  grant_id: "range-1",
  audiences: ["https://compute.example"],
  issued_at: now,
  not_before: now,
  expires_at: now + 3_600,
  holder_thumbprint: thumbprint,
  operations: [
    %V2.Operation{
      name: "allocate_workers",
      selectors: [
        {:gte, ["workers"], {:integer, 1}},
        {:lte, ["workers"], {:integer, 8}}
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{:ok, input} = V2.grant_signing_input(grant, %{})
compact = sign.(input, issuer_private)
:range_grant_signed
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:range_grant_signed
```

## Sign a proof for each request

Keep the grant fixed while signing a proof for each worker value. The expectation uses the
same server-derived operation and arguments, isolating the selector result.

```elixir
check = fn tagged ->
  arguments = {:object, [{"workers", tagged}]}

  proof = %V2.Proof{
    holder_public_key: holder_public,
    proof_id: "range-proof",
    method: "POST",
    target_uri: "https://compute.example/jobs",
    issued_at: now,
    nonce: nil,
    invocation_id: "7d444840-9dc0-11ed-a8fc-0242ac120002",
    operation: "allocate_workers",
    grant_compact: compact,
    cast_arguments: arguments
  }

  {:ok, proof_input} = V2.proof_signing_input(proof, %{})
  credentials = %Credentials{grant: compact, proof: sign.(proof_input, holder_private)}

  expected = %ExpectedRequest{
    trusted_issuer: %TrustedIssuer{key_id: "issuer-1", public_key: issuer_public},
    issuer: "https://issuer.example",
    audience: "https://compute.example",
    method: "POST",
    target_uri: "https://compute.example/jobs",
    invocation_id: "7d444840-9dc0-11ed-a8fc-0242ac120002",
    operation: "allocate_workers",
    cast_arguments: arguments,
    evaluation_time: now,
    clock_skew: 0,
    proof_max_age: 300,
    nonce: :not_required,
    bounds: %{}
  }

  V2.check_envelope(credentials, expected)
end

:request_check_ready
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:request_check_ready
```

## Verify the inclusive endpoints

One and eight workers are both inside the grant. Successful facts still carry no execution
decision.

```elixir
{:ok, lower} = check.({:integer, 1})
{:ok, upper} = check.({:integer, 8})

%{lower: lower.authorization, upper: upper.authorization}
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{lower: :not_evaluated, upper: :not_evaluated}
```

## Refuse out-of-range values and the wrong tag

Zero and nine fall outside the bounds. A float-tagged one is also refused, despite having the
same numeric value as the lower integer bound.

```elixir
{:error, :invalid} = check.({:integer, 0})
{:error, :invalid} = check.({:integer, 9})
{:error, :invalid} = check.({:float, 1.0})

[:below_minimum, :above_maximum, :numeric_tag_mismatch]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[:below_minimum, :above_maximum, :numeric_tag_mismatch]
```

## Refuse invalid selectors before signing

Range bounds must be numeric and paths must name a member. These malformed inputs never
produce bytes for an issuer to sign.

```elixir
string_bound = %V2.Operation{
  name: "allocate_workers",
  selectors: [{:lte, ["workers"], {:string, "8"}}]
}

empty_path = %V2.Operation{
  name: "allocate_workers",
  selectors: [{:gte, [], {:integer, 1}}]
}

{:error, :invalid} = V2.grant_signing_input(%{grant | operations: [string_bound]}, %{})
{:error, :invalid} = V2.grant_signing_input(%{grant | operations: [empty_path]}, %{})

[:string_bound, :empty_path]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[:string_bound, :empty_path]
```

See [tagged arguments and selectors](../guides/tagged-arguments-and-selectors.md) for the
closed selector algebra and integer/float rules.
